{"word": "indicator", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "indicator", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɪn.dɪˌkeɪ.tə(ɹ)/", "tags": ["UK"]}], "forms": [{"word": "indicators", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "A pointer or index that indicates something.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The visual indicators are a decided improvement, and have reduced the telephoning by the staff in the signalbox.", "reference": "1954 November, “Train Arrival Indicators at Euston”, in Railway Magazine, page 738:"}, {"text": "These old indicators [at Earl's Court station] show the destination of the next train by the appearance of an illuminated arrow next to the station name. The arrow may indicate that a train is going to Wimbledon, but it gives no clue as to when. Now, however, the indicators are supplemented by dot matrix panels that not only say where but also when. Why aren't the old ones removed? Because they, like the whole station, are Grade II listed.", "reference": "2012, Andrew Martin, Underground Overground: A passenger's history of the Tube, Profile Books, →ISBN, page 79:"}, {"text": "The phrase was, and remains, a double-edged sword: a testament to the hard-working and hard-living citizens of the Crescent City and an indicator of the “ah, whatever” shrug hanging over its corrupt politics, its ramshackle infrastructure and its belief that partying trumps all.", "reference": "2015 August 12, Todd Leopold, “Return to the ‘City That Care Forgot’”, in CNN, archived from the original on 02 Jan 2022:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "показалец"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "стрелка"}, {"language": {"code": "cmn", "name": "Chinese Mandarin"}, "word": "指示符"}, {"language": {"code": "nl", "name": "Dutch"}, "word": "richtingaanwijzer"},